A||N||4||Dan87GT||V6 Thermostat won't open||||1||
Z||000000||Dan87GT||11-06-2001||06:56 AM||||I just replaced the engine in my 86SE and the temperature keeps pegging and finally the thermostat will open. I have chaged the thermostat 3 times, made sure the system is full etc. Thihs has also happened to me several times in the winter.. Any thoughts????||||reg||1
Z||000001||Carrolles||11-06-2001||08:08 AM||||Sounds like the classic air bubble floating around in the cooling system.<P>You say that you removed the air.  Did you use the recommended procedure?<P>It doesn't take a large air bubble (maybe a pint or smaller) to air lock the system.<p>[This message has been edited by Carrolles (edited 11-06-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||Indyrestoguy_JR||11-06-2001||09:29 AM||||Give the temp sensor for the gauge a function check. When this happens, does the radiator fan come on?<BR>If it doesn't, I would imagine that the temp sensor is just bad.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Oreif||11-06-2001||10:02 AM||||Burp the system. Do when engine is cool.<BR>Raise back of car slightly. <BR>Remove thermostat cap on engine.<BR>Remove thermostat.<BR>SLOWLY remove radiator cap in front. You should hear air hissing out then coolant leaking. (If coolant doesn't leak out you are way low on coolant.)<BR>If coolant leaks out front re-cap and fill from back. If you have no coolant leak out,<BR>leave cap off, fill from back until front overflows. Cap radiator then continue to fill from rear.<BR>Replace thermostat and cap.<BR>You should be good to go.<P><BR>------------------<BR>             <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg">           <BR><FONT SIZE="1"><B><I>Happiness isn't around the corner...<BR>Happiness IS the corner.</I></B></FONT><p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 11-06-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||I'mSmall||11-06-2001||11:28 AM||||One thing I did was take the thermostat out and place it on a table and drill 2 holes across from each other with a small drill bit. If you do this make sure to take any/all burrs off so they dont come off in your cooling system. <BR>  As for the other reply, one way to see if there is an air bubble is when your car over heats wait until it is semi-cool or even all the way cool, and take off your thermo-cap and your thermostat, if there is no coolant in the hole you have an air bubble. Be carefull doing this, escaping hot air/coolant will burn the f@#* (  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"> ) out of you.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> <p>[This message has been edited by I'mSmall (edited 11-06-2001).]||||reg||10
